George Steinbrenner was arguably the  most impactful owner to come through  baseball he built the Yankees into the  financial Behemoth they are today the  most valuable team in baseball valued at  6 billion according to our estimates he  led them the seven World Series  countless Marquee free agent signings  Steinbrenner never hesitated to bring  stars to the Bronx from Reggie Jackson  to Alex Rodriguez he always pushed to  keep the Yankees competitive and his win  at all cost mentality could be  detrimental at times especially how he  was perceived in the public eye but  ultimately he put a winner on the field  many times on Seinfeld we quit a glimpse  of what was supposed to be the back of  George steinburner's head he was  parodied on the popular sitcom and it  was an enjoyable exaggeration that  reflected how he was this Larger than  Life Persona I remember the first time  the character appears is when George  Costanza is following the opposite of  his instincts and interviewing for a job  with the Yankees and he runs in the  Steinbrenner and ends up insulting him  and the line is something like hire this  man foreign and I think that's just  representative of how kind of  temperamental Steinbrenner was and how  fiery he was and just how much of a  larger than life Persona he had become  he was passionate but he was also  erratic you know you look back at some  of the things that happened during his  tenure he hired and refired manager  Billy Martin several times he went  through a number of publicists during  the early years of his tenure and also  he had some more complicated issues in  his regime he was kicked out of baseball  twice the first for making illegal  campaign contributions which I should  say that he was later pardoned for but  also he later made Shady business  dealings in the 90s that resulted in  another Breakaway from the game well  more so his day-to-day management of the  team when George passed away in 2010 he  was survived by his wife and his four  children Hal Hank Jessica and Jennifer  his wife later passed away but  coincidentally he died in a year where  there was no estate tax so if he had  passed away months later and the new  rate of 55 had kicked in which it did in  January of 2011 his heirs could have  been on the hook for something like 600  million in the time since George's death  the Yankees have grown tremendously in  value from 1.6 billion in 2010 to 6  billion today that's been the primary  driver of growth for this family's  fortune and we estimate that each of  George's kids got an equal share  Hank unfortunately George's eldest son  died in 2020 so it's unclear what  happened to his share of the fortune but  we estimate that his three surviving  kids Hal Jessica and Jennifer based on  their controlling interests in Yankee  Global Enterprises which is the holding  company of the Yankees makes them all  billionaires Under the Umbrella of  Yankee Global Enterprises the holding  company that controls the Yankees there  are several other businesses the  Steinbrenner family has pieces of one is  the Yes Network which is the network  that George actually came together with  the Nets and created to broadcast Yankee  games regionally there's also New York  City FC the major league soccer club  that plays in Yankee Stadium as well as  Legends Hospitality a joint venture that  Steinbrenner came together with Dallas  Cowboys owner Jerry Jones to form in  2008 and then more recently Yankee  Global Enterprises took part in Redbird  capital's acquisition of Italian soccer  club AC Milan were reported 1.28 billion  in terms of how the heirs compared  George's oversight the Yankees it's  definitely a more quiet and tranquil  regime  how is the guy who runs the team now and  he's not necessarily the outspoken  person that his father was or even that  his older brother Hank was  but that doesn't mean they've lost their  reputation for spending in 2017 they  traded for John Carlos Stanton to Star  outfielder who had signed a 13-year 325  million dollar contract with the Marlins  two years later they went out and  shelled out another 324 million signing  Garrett Cole to a nine-year deal the  most ever for a major league pitcher  success is kind of lagged though for the  franchise they're in the midst of a  World Series drought over the last 13  years the last time they won was 2009  when George was still alive and they  haven't been back to the fall classic  since  so you could say that the Yankees  haven't achieved what they've wanted to  in that time but also they've been  competitive on the field nearly every  year they were back in it winning 99  games this year so it depends how you  define success but the Yankee brand  demands success  and not reaching the World Series  especially over the last few years is  something that definitely weighs heavily  on the franchise
 

